  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If ${log}_{4}⁡x=3$, then $x=$",
  "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use a base of $4$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡x & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{3} \\\\ x & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
